diff --git a/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md b/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md
index b7d3306b1..b40f8be4a 100644
--- a/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md
+++ b/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md
@@ -5428,6 +5428,13 @@ First-city scouting efficiency follow-up:
 - Before each further generic scouting step, the settler now compares its current plot and other revealed reachable sites by complete found value minus `SAS_AI_FOUND_FIRST_CITY_RETURN_TRAVEL_VALUE_PER_TURN` for each return-path turn. If another step would make the best return exceed the search window, it commits to that site; movement and founding are queued together so the settler cannot restart scouting on arrival. The current plot is included so a strong site such as Aztec `(34,24)` is not abandoned for a weaker return target.
 - Final regression testing preserved Berlin `(33,13)` on turn 6 in save file 442. Karakorum committed on turn 5 to `(50,40)` two turns away in save file 360, and Cuzco committed on turn 5 to `(38,44)` two turns away instead of founding around turn 10 in save file 431. The same save file 431 test kept the Aztec capital at `(34,24)` and founded it on turn 6; no return/restart loops occurred.
 
+Later save-file-442 Berlin return regression:
+
+- In a later run, Berlin's settler wandered from its `(35,10)` start to `(39,19)`. At turn 6, the deadline check still saw strong `(33,13)` at raw found value `3961`, but because it was now five return turns away, the travel adjustment let nearby `(41,18)` win despite its lower raw value of `3640`; Berlin then founded there.
+- The deadline now protects the highest raw found-value reachable site and uses return cost only to break an exact tie. This makes the settler turn back before its own outbound wandering can progressively replace the strongest known capital site with a weaker nearby endpoint. BBAI retesting with save file 442 fixed Berlin by founding at `(33,13)` on turn 7 instead of `(41,18)`. The post-window safety fallback still uses travel-adjusted value when choosing between returning sites.
+- Detailed regression testing then preserved Karakorum at `(50,40)` in save file 360, but exposed the opposite Cuzco case in save file 431. Cuzco had deliberately left poor `(41,46)` to keep scouting; at the deadline, that fully revealed origin still scored `3488`, while nearby `(38,44)` scored only `3355` with four BFC plots still unrevealed. Strict raw priority pulled the settler back to `(41,46)`.
+- Raw priority therefore protects a newly discovered best site, as in Berlin and Karakorum, but not the abandoned scout origin itself. When the raw winner is that origin, sites are compared after subtracting their return-path cost so nearby scouting can finish rather than being undone. Final three-map BBAI retesting preserved Berlin at `(33,13)` in save file 442 and Karakorum at `(50,40)` in save file 360, and restored Cuzco to `(38,44)` in save file 431.
+
 ## 145 - (Implemented / needs in-game test) Military Advisor Map tab lost selected leaders after tab switch or close/reopen
 
 In the Military Advisor Map tab, selecting another leader was not preserved when switching to another Military Advisor tab, returning to the Map tab, or closing and reopening the advisor. The Map tab leader selection was reset to the active player each time.

diff --git a/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md b/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md
index 8813df0a5..0ccc16ee3 100644
--- a/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md
+++ b/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md
@@ -314,7 +314,7 @@ Note 2: Master–vassal(s) changes are intended to strengthen overall AI play by
 - **Free window:** during the first ~75 turns at Normal speed (scaled by game speed), a "free to produce settler" period lets the capital grow before switching into chosen early strategy (offense, defense, etc.).
 - **First city logic:** settlers consider a larger no-penalty scouting radius for founding the capital (`CvUnitAI::AI_foundFirstCity`), and no longer auto-reject non-coastal/non-river sites if a worse "preferred" site exists. It’ll just pick the **best** overall site. See [KI#43](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#43---attemptingly-improvedenhanced-ai-settlers-for-the-first-city-found-ie-at-turn-0-if-i-am-not-mistaken-settling-too-soon-instead-of-digging-a-bit-for-better-sites-and-other-related-changes).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5940+) **First-city roaming**: Since the first city is especially important, unlike later cities that can be more strategic or semi-decent resource grabs, starting settlers can now **roam briefly for a better first-city site when the current site is bad enough**. Settlers can spend the bounded early window to **move to visible good-enough sites**, do **safe adjacent scouting for better information**, or **wait if no safe scouting step exists**. (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5976+) Starting-site checks use a shared **potential-food environment score** (using the same logic described under Low-food environments). Food-surplus plots offset poor plots, productive Grass Hills remain neutral, and no-yield peaks/ice receive an extra citizen-unworkable penalty because they are dead BFC slots. This keeps useful resources/coasts from looking poor while still moving away from bad tundra/plains/peak-heavy starts.
-- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6009+) **First-city recheck and efficient return after scouting:** After leaving a very poor spawn location, the AI settler explores nearby unrevealed plots (fog) within a limited scouting window (in turns) instead of founding the first merely good-enough site, trying to find better first-city sites, like a human player would. Only fully revealed BFCs can interrupt scouting; before each further step, the AI settler compares its current plot and other revealed reachable sites by full city-site value minus a tunable return-path cost. The turns window includes the trip back, so the AI settler commits before further scouting would make its best return too late, then queues movement and founding together instead of restarting scouting on arrival or founding wherever scouting stopped. Empirically and as BBAI logging also showed, this helped Berlin, Karakorum, Cuzco, and the Aztecs choose strong revealed sites and found promptly in save files 442, 360, and 431. As of now this applies only to the first city, which critically benefits from extra information and founds much stronger first-city sites, while later AI settlers usually know more of the local map and so do not need to use this (so as of now disabled for them). See [KI#144](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#144---fixed-base-advciv-issue-and-advciv-sas-settler-free-window-follow-up-ai-settlers-sometimes-do-not-move-away-from-a-high-bad-plot-count-start-eg-high-non-bonus-tundra-and-plains-they-now-scout-and-hunt-for-better-not-very-bad-sites-and-no-longer-stop-at-first-good-enough-site-but-instead-now-rerun-evaluate-city-site-again-on-newly-visible-plots-if-a-better-site-eg-more-foodriversfresh-water-exists-nearby-which-we-now-value-more-too-for-first-city-as-well).
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6009+) **First-city recheck and efficient return after scouting:** After leaving a very poor spawn location, the AI settler explores nearby unrevealed plots (fog) within a limited scouting window (in turns) instead of founding the first merely good-enough site, trying to find better first-city sites, like a human player would. Only fully revealed BFCs can interrupt scouting; before each further step, the AI settler protects the strongest newly discovered reachable site by full city-site value, with the tunable return-path cost breaking exact ties. If the raw-value winner is instead the poor scout origin it deliberately left, sites are compared after subtracting their return-path cost so a nearby partly revealed candidate can finish being explored instead of needlessly undoing the scouting trip. The turns window includes the trip back, so the AI settler commits before further scouting would make its strongest return too late, then queues movement and founding together instead of restarting scouting on arrival or founding wherever scouting stopped. Once this window has ended, return-path cost can still favor a nearly equal nearby site over lengthy backtracking. Empirically and as BBAI logging also showed, this helped Berlin, Karakorum, Cuzco, and the Aztecs choose strong revealed sites and found promptly in save files 442, 360, and 431. As of now this applies only to the first city, which critically benefits from extra information and founds much stronger first-city sites, while later AI settlers usually know more of the local map and so do not need to use this (so as of now disabled for them). See [KI#144](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#144---fixed-base-advciv-issue-and-advciv-sas-settler-free-window-follow-up-ai-settlers-sometimes-do-not-move-away-from-a-high-bad-plot-count-start-eg-high-non-bonus-tundra-and-plains-they-now-scout-and-hunt-for-better-not-very-bad-sites-and-no-longer-stop-at-first-good-enough-site-but-instead-now-rerun-evaluate-city-site-again-on-newly-visible-plots-if-a-better-site-eg-more-foodriversfresh-water-exists-nearby-which-we-now-value-more-too-for-first-city-as-well).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5928+) **Optional naval-heavy-map coastal settling bias:** Naval-heavy maps can now add a tunable first-city or later-city preference for coastal settling in naval-heavy maps. This should be more useful and strategically stronger in such maps, notably for coastal access, work boats, scouting/contact, trade, first galleys, Moai, or other water-focused strategies. However, following the [KI#173](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#173---fixedimproved-base-advciv-issue-live-first-settler-scoring-reused-hidden-map-generation-surroundings) and related AI settler/found scoring changes, BBAI logging showed even a value of `0` correctly selects the strong Cuzco coastal site in save file 420, while Japan no longer favors the old tundra-heavy candidates that motivated this new feature. So use only a fairly small naval settling bias positive value for additional strategic coastal preference. See [KI#146](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#146---fixedenhanced-base-advciv-issue-of-ai-undervaluing-coastal-settling-on-naval-heavy-maps).
 - **Parking addressed (early game):** Reduced cases of settlers idling in the capital despite safe/urgent expansion, by updating `CvUnitAI::AI_found` to found sooner when conditions allow. In autoplay samples, Spain shifted from **1 city @T100** to **~2 by T60–70** and **~3 by T80–90** (settler production varies somehow, but we move much sooner after we have it now). See [KI#68](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#68---tremendously-improved-ai-having-a-settler-parked-from-turn---45-to-turn-100-and-still-didnt-found-any-city-fixedaddressed-in-cvunitaiai_found).

